//         "data": "<h2>Understanding the Basics of Physics</h2>\n<p>Physics is a branch of science that deals with the study of the natural world around us, focusing on concepts such as motion, force, and energy. In Grade 7, the physics syllabus is integrated into the broader science curriculum, covering essential topics that help students understand how the world works.</p>\n\n### Chapter 4: Heat\n<h3>Introduction to Heat</h3>\n<p>This chapter introduces students to the concept of heat, including how to measure temperature and the transfer of heat. Students learn about laboratory thermometers, the kinds of clothes we wear in different seasons, and the principles of radiation.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n<p>Students will learn about:\n- Measuring temperature\n- Laboratory thermometers\n- Transfer of heat\n- Types of clothes for different seasons\n- Radiation</p>\n\n<Examples>\n<p>Examples include understanding why we wear lighter clothes in summer and heavier clothes in winter, and how thermometers work to measure temperature.</p>\n\n**** Image description ****\nA diagram showing the structure of a laboratory thermometer and how it measures temperature, along with illustrations of different types of clothing for summer and winter.\n\n<Practice Questions>\n<p>1. What is the primary function of a laboratory thermometer?\n2. Why do we wear lighter clothes in summer?\n3. What is radiation in the context of heat?</p>\n\n<Summary>\n<p>This chapter provides a foundational understanding of heat, temperature measurement, and the practical applications of these concepts in everyday life.</p>\n\n### Chapter 8: Winds, Storms and Cyclones\n<h3>Understanding Natural Calamities</h3>\n<p>This chapter delves into the science behind natural calamities such as winds, storms, and cyclones. Students learn about air pressure, the causes of high-speed winds, uneven heating of the Earth, and safety measures against cyclones.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n<p>Students will learn about:\n- Air pressure and its effects\n- Causes of high-speed winds\n- Uneven heating of the Earth\n- Cyclones and safety measures\n- Thunderstorms, hurricanes, typhoons, and tornadoes</p>\n\n<Examples>\n<p>Examples include understanding how air expands when heated, the formation of cyclones, and the differences between various types of storms.</p>\n\n**** Image description ****\nA diagram illustrating the formation of cyclones, showing the low-pressure area at the center and the wind flow patterns. Another diagram comparing different types of storms like hurricanes, typhoons, and tornadoes.\n\n<Practice Questions>\n<p>1. What causes high-speed winds?\n2. How are cyclones formed?\n3. What are the safety measures against cyclones?</p>\n\n<Summary>\n<p>This chapter helps students understand the scientific principles behind natural calamities and how to prepare for them.</p>\n\n### Chapter 13: Motion and Time\n<h3>Understanding Motion</h3>\n<p>This chapter introduces students to the fundamentals of motion, including speed, measurement of time, and different types of motion. Students learn about distance-time graphs, uniform and non-uniform motion, and the simple pendulum.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n<p>Students will learn about:\n- Examples of motion around us\n- Speed and its measurement\n- Units of time and speed\n- Distance-time graphs\n- Uniform and non-uniform motion\n- Simple pendulum</p>\n\n<Examples>\n<p>Examples include understanding how to calculate speed, interpreting distance-time graphs, and observing the motion of a simple pendulum.</p>\n\n**** Image description ****\nA diagram showing a distance-time graph and an illustration of a simple pendulum in motion.\n\n<Practice Questions>\n<p>1. How is speed calculated?\n2. What is the difference between uniform and non-uniform motion?\n3. What is a simple pendulum?</p>\n\n<Summary>\n<p>This chapter provides a comprehensive understanding of motion, time, and their measurements.</p>\n\n### Chapter 14: Electric Current and its Effects\n<h3>Introduction to Electric Current</h3>\n<p>This chapter introduces students to the basics of electric current, including symbols of electronic components, the heating and magnetic effects of electric current, and simple circuits.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n<p>Students will learn about:\n- Symbols of electronic components (e.g., electric cell, bulb, switch)\n- Heating effect of electric current\n- Magnetic effect of electric current\n- Simple circuit diagrams\n- Electromagnets and electric bells</p>\n\n<Examples>\n<p>Examples include understanding how electric cells and bulbs work, the heating effect of electric current, and the construction of simple circuits.</p>\n\n**** Image description ****\nA diagram showing the symbols of electronic components and a simple circuit diagram with an electric cell, bulb, and switch.\n\n<Practice Questions>\n<p>1. What are the symbols for common electronic components?\n2. What is the heating effect of electric current?\n3. How does an electromagnet work?</p>\n\n<Summary>\n<p>This chapter introduces students to the fundamental concepts of electric current and its practical applications.</p>\n\n### Chapter 15: Light\n<h3>Understanding Light</h3>\n<p>This chapter explores the nature of light, including reflection, refraction, and the properties of mirrors and lenses. Students learn about concave and convex mirrors, spherical mirrors, and the formation of images.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n<p>Students will learn about:\n- Light traveling in a straight line\n- Reflection of light\n- Concave and convex mirrors\n- Lenses and image formation\n- Color of sunlight and prisms</p>\n\n<Examples>\n<p>Examples include understanding how mirrors reflect light, the use of lenses to focus light, and the formation of images using mirrors and lenses.</p>\n\n**** Image description ****\nA diagram illustrating the reflection of light by concave and convex mirrors, and another showing the formation of images using lenses.\n\n<Practice Questions>\n<p>1. How does light travel?\n2. What is the difference between concave and convex mirrors?\n3. How do lenses form images?</p>\n\n<Summary>\n<p>This chapter provides a detailed understanding of the properties and behaviors of light.</p>"
